  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish that temperature-dependent deformation of closely related FCC HEAs can alter dislocation density, stacking faults, FCC-HCP transformation, strength, and elastic stiffness. In particular, R1 and R8 connect high temperature with reduced or annihilated dislocation structures and increased HCP fraction, while R9 and R16 show elevated-temperature elastic softening. Several records also use molecular-dynamics deformation or nanoindentation. However, none combines a FeNiCrCoCu coating on Cu with temperature-controlled nanoindentation and sliding friction, nor predicts the specific transition near 900 K from stacking-fault-mediated deformation to lattice disorder and the associated increase in friction. Thus the literature points in the right mechanistic direction, but the specific intervention and tribological linkage require a substantial new idea."
